The reviewer is right that PGM IV 296–466 does not show that the whole set (figure, bed, lamps, herbal juice, spell) was 'standard equipment'. But the proposed fix drops a parallel the papyrus does support, and it adds clay. Checked in Preisendanz, Papyri Graecae Magicae I (Teubner 1928; archive.org papyrigraecaemag0001unse_x4f6, Claremont library scan).

(1) What the spell prescribes, p. 82, IV 296–297: 'Φιλτροκατάδεσμος θαυμαστός· λαβὼν κηρὸν ⟨ἢ πηλὸν⟩ ἀπὸ τροχοῦ κεραμικοῦ πλάσον ζῴδια δύο, ἀρρενικὸν καὶ θηλυκόν'. German translation, p. 83: 'Wunderbarer Liebeszwang. Nimm Wachs ‹oder Ton› von einer Töpferscheibe und knete zwei Figuren, eine männliche und eine weibliche.'

(2) The 'or clay' is an editorial supplement. Apparatus, p. 82: '296 κηρον P, was Rieß hält; πηλὸν Kt'. So the papyrus itself reads wax, and 'wax figures' in our note is accurate.

(3) The name is written on the figure, p. 82, IV 317–318: 'ἐπὶ δὲ τοῦ στήθους τὸ ὄνομα τῆς ἀγομένης μητρόθεν'. German, p. 83: 'auf die Brust den Namen der Beigezwungenen nach ihrer Mutter'. This parallels 'nomen reginae adscribens'. Magical words are also written on the other body parts.

(4) The rest of the recipe, pp. 82–89:
- thirteen bronze needles stuck into the figure;
- the same spell written on a lead tablet ('ὁ λόγος ὁ γραφόμενος καὶ διωκόμενος', IV 335) and recited;
- 365 knots;
- the figures deposited 'ἡλίου δύνοντος παρὰ ἀώρου ἢ βιαίου θήκην' (IV 333–334);
- the binding logos, then a prayer to Helios at sunset (IV 436 ff.).
There is no bed, no lamp and no herbal juice. The magical material (οὐσία) is tied to the figure's head or neck (IV 303–304).

So the verifiable common ground is: a wax figure, the victim's name written on it, and a spoken spell. The proposed wording keeps exactly that and says what is absent.
